1
00:00:04,295 --> 00:00:05,546
{\an8}"All right, how is that?"

2
00:00:26,692 --> 00:00:29,028
- You jackass sons of bitches!
- What the hell?

3
00:00:29,278 --> 00:00:31,239
Don't think I don't know
what your game is.

4
00:00:31,864 --> 00:00:32,907
What's wrong with you?

5
00:00:33,324 --> 00:00:35,535
If I wasn't in a hurry,
I'd kick your lousy butts.

6
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:41,541
Hey, where you going?

7
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:43,793
Somebody stop him!

8
00:00:44,669 --> 00:00:45,711
"Call 911!"

9
00:00:56,556 --> 00:00:57,598
Good morning.

10
00:00:59,475 --> 00:01:00,518
Hey.

11
00:01:01,352 --> 00:01:03,729
Mr. Whitaker.
Mr. Whitaker!

12
00:01:04,564 --> 00:01:05,606
Sir!

13
00:01:07,733 --> 00:01:11,153
"His name's Charles Whitaker,
32, single."

14
00:01:11,654 --> 00:01:14,073
He's a computer engineer
specializing in mapmaking.

15
00:01:14,782 --> 00:01:17,535
This is his workplace in Houston
yesterday morning.

16
00:01:17,743 --> 00:01:19,328
He's saying,
"Stop. Listen."

17
00:01:19,954 --> 00:01:20,997
"Help me."

18
00:01:23,541 --> 00:01:24,667
That was the last thing he said.

19
00:01:26,961 --> 00:01:28,713
- I take it he's dead.
- Heart failure

20
00:01:28,838 --> 00:01:32,800
Induced by a massive overdose
of prescription and illegal drugs.

21
00:01:33,509 --> 00:01:35,636
Hm. So, he OD'd
and freaked out.

22
00:01:35,761 --> 00:01:37,305
Why is the FBI
concerned with this?

23
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:39,682
He had sleeping pills,
crystal meth, MDMA

24
00:01:39,807 --> 00:01:41,475
and horse tranquilizers
in his system

25
00:01:41,601 --> 00:01:44,145
but, there were no pill bottles
or other drug paraphernalia

26
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:45,313
anywhere in his home.

27
00:01:45,438 --> 00:01:46,856
He had
no drug history whatsoever.

28
00:01:46,981 --> 00:01:49,817
His security profile indicates
no suicidal tendencies.

29
00:01:49,942 --> 00:01:53,070
Accident or suicide is probable,
but murder is a possibility.

30
00:01:53,362 --> 00:01:55,406
He was definitely trying to say
something before he died.

31
00:01:55,531 --> 00:01:58,451
Why OD
and then decide to talk?

32
00:01:58,576 --> 00:01:59,994
And why does he have
a security profile?

33
00:02:00,119 --> 00:02:01,162
Aye, there's the rub.

34
00:02:01,287 --> 00:02:02,830
That's why we're looking
into him at all.

35
00:02:03,289 --> 00:02:04,332
Uh...

36
00:02:05,875 --> 00:02:07,752
Oh. Wait here.

37
00:02:10,921 --> 00:02:12,840
This room makes me feel like
I'm in an aquarium

38
00:02:12,965 --> 00:02:14,091
and everyone's looking at me.

39
00:02:14,216 --> 00:02:16,636
Mm. Well, you could
very easily feel like

40
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:18,763
they're all in the aquarium
and you're looking at them.

41
00:02:22,016 --> 00:02:24,644
- What are you doing?
- I'm following the conversation.

42
00:02:24,935 --> 00:02:27,563
The G-man says that I don't have
adequate security clearance

43
00:02:27,688 --> 00:02:30,900
to work on the Whitaker case,
and Fischer is arguing.

44
00:02:31,067 --> 00:02:32,109
Are you reading their lips?

45
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,528
Yeah, I would be if I didn't
have to answer inane questions.

46
00:02:34,654 --> 00:02:35,696
Oh, okay, boss.

47
00:02:35,821 --> 00:02:37,323
I'm not your boss.
Why would you say that?

48
00:02:37,448 --> 00:02:39,408
It's just that sometimes now
you act like you are.

49
00:02:39,784 --> 00:02:41,535
But we're equals, partners.

50
00:02:41,661 --> 00:02:43,371
Okay, fine.
Just remember that.

51
00:02:43,496 --> 00:02:45,122
I will remember that.
I... I will.

52
00:02:45,539 --> 00:02:47,249
Oh, great. Now I don't know
what they decided.

53
00:02:47,375 --> 00:02:48,918
- Hold on, sir.
- I'm done discussing this.

54
00:02:49,585 --> 00:02:51,754
Jane, I'm Supervising Agent Daniels.

55
00:02:51,879 --> 00:02:54,090
I'm in charge of security
clearance for the office.

56
00:02:54,298 --> 00:02:56,967
I need you to leave immediately.
This briefing is classified.

57
00:02:57,093 --> 00:02:59,261
Sir, I am in the process
of getting him clearance

58
00:02:59,387 --> 00:03:01,055
from D.C. with an FD-258.

59
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:02,515
Jane is a consultant.

60
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:05,226
The FD-258 grants temporary
security clearance

61
00:03:05,351 --> 00:03:08,104
to local officials in the event
of a terrorist attack.

62
00:03:08,229 --> 00:03:10,106
- He hardly qualifies.
- You're right.

63
00:03:10,523 --> 00:03:12,650
And, uh, if Whitaker was murdered

64
00:03:12,775 --> 00:03:16,028
because of his highly sensitive
military work with the Pentagon

65
00:03:16,153 --> 00:03:17,780
I shouldn't know
anything about that.

66
00:03:17,905 --> 00:03:19,740
So, I'll happily leave.

67
00:03:20,324 --> 00:03:21,367
Wait.

68
00:03:22,785 --> 00:03:24,412
- What did you tell him?
- Nothing.

69
00:03:24,537 --> 00:03:26,664
Who told you
that he works for the Pentagon?

70
00:03:26,789 --> 00:03:28,416
And consider
your answer well.

71
00:03:30,668 --> 00:03:32,670
- You did.
- Don't be absurd.

72
00:03:33,129 --> 00:03:34,839
I've never seen you before.

73
00:03:34,964 --> 00:03:36,465
No, but your presence here
told me

74
00:03:36,590 --> 00:03:38,884
because you wouldn't be here
if he worked for, uh,

75
00:03:39,135 --> 00:03:40,845
Pottery Shack or Pizza Barn,
would you?

76
00:03:40,970 --> 00:03:42,304
- Excuse me?
- You're excused.

77
00:03:42,722 --> 00:03:44,348
He's a little slow
on the uptake, this one.

78
00:03:44,473 --> 00:03:46,183
Fischer, this is unacceptable.

79
00:03:46,308 --> 00:03:49,061
- I apologize, sir.
- No, no. Hush. Don't apologize.

80
00:03:49,186 --> 00:03:51,021
Don't take it out on her.
She didn't do anything.

81
00:03:52,273 --> 00:03:55,943
Good morning, everybody.
What's all the rumpus?

82
00:03:56,068 --> 00:03:58,279
I was just explaining
to Agent Fischer

83
00:03:58,404 --> 00:04:01,198
that Mr. Jane doesn't have
security clearance

84
00:04:01,323 --> 00:04:02,366
for the Whitaker case.

85
00:04:02,491 --> 00:04:04,452
Oh. Well, problem solved.

86
00:04:04,577 --> 00:04:07,121
I just talked to the Deputy Director
and, uh, he approved.

87
00:04:07,246 --> 00:04:08,706
And you got the clearance
in writing?

88
00:04:08,831 --> 00:04:09,874
Yes, sir.

89
00:04:11,542 --> 00:04:14,253
Well, then,
I guess all this is on you.

90
00:04:14,962 --> 00:04:16,005
Mm-hmm.

91
00:04:16,130 --> 00:04:17,173
Carry on.

92
00:04:19,675 --> 00:04:21,719
- Thanks, boss.
- No problem.

93
00:04:22,094 --> 00:04:24,972
Congratulations, Jane.
You now have full security clearance.

94
00:04:25,097 --> 00:04:26,390
Very few civilians get it.

95
00:04:27,099 --> 00:04:28,976
That is awesome.
Thank you so much.

96
00:04:29,101 --> 00:04:31,395
Oh, oh. Okay. Area 51.

97
00:04:31,812 --> 00:04:33,898
What's the real story, huh?
Give me the skinny.

98
00:04:52,039 --> 00:04:54,041
How long had your brother
lived here, Mr. Whitaker?

99
00:04:54,458 --> 00:04:56,084
Uh, Curtis, please.

100
00:04:57,044 --> 00:04:58,670
I believe it was
four years or so.

101
00:05:00,422 --> 00:05:01,465
Do you think it was murder?

102
00:05:01,590 --> 00:05:04,218
Uh, surely, it was suicide. How could
someone feed him all those pills?

103
00:05:04,384 --> 00:05:05,469
Well, that's a good question.

104
00:05:06,011 --> 00:05:07,805
{\an8}- Any guesses?
- No.

105
00:05:09,389 --> 00:05:11,600
{\an8}Did your brother have a drug
problem, to your knowledge?

106
00:05:12,017 --> 00:05:14,228
{\an8}No, never. He was straight
edge his whole life.

107
00:05:15,270 --> 00:05:16,980
{\an8}But for heaven's sake,
why would anyone murder him?

108
00:05:17,147 --> 00:05:18,607
{\an8}Well, there's another
good question.

109
00:05:18,816 --> 00:05:20,526
{\an8}- Any guesses on that one?
- No.

110
00:05:21,068 --> 00:05:22,361
{\an8}I'm not sure I like your manner.

111
00:05:23,904 --> 00:05:26,782
That is a very nice suit.

112
00:05:27,449 --> 00:05:29,159
{\an8}I always liked
the double-breasted, but it's...

113
00:05:29,284 --> 00:05:30,369
{\an8}Doesn't work on me.
I'm too short.

114
00:05:30,494 --> 00:05:31,537
Did you get that in London?

115
00:05:31,662 --> 00:05:33,664
{\an8}- Yes, as a matter of fact.
- Mm.

116
00:05:34,081 --> 00:05:37,751
{\an8}You know, usually, uh, money
is either earned or inherited.

117
00:05:37,876 --> 00:05:40,212
{\an8}- Yours is both, isn't it?
- I suppose.

118
00:05:41,004 --> 00:05:43,465
{\an8}The family is established.
I'm in banking.

119
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:44,633
{\an8}Bless you for your service.

120
00:05:44,758 --> 00:05:47,469
{\an8}And your brother drew maps,
nothing fancy.

121
00:05:47,594 --> 00:05:49,221
Well, compared to the rest
of the family.

122
00:05:49,346 --> 00:05:50,556
{\an8}I did not say that.

123
00:05:51,139 --> 00:05:53,725
{\an8}My brother was extremely talented
but he went his own way.

124
00:05:54,643 --> 00:05:56,311
{\an8}My family values achievement.

125
00:05:57,062 --> 00:05:58,105
{\an8}Charles was a dreamer.

126
00:05:59,189 --> 00:06:02,568
{\an8}Uh, did you notice anything unusual
about your brother's behavior recently?

127
00:06:02,693 --> 00:06:03,777
Full disclosure, there...

128
00:06:04,069 --> 00:06:06,446
{\an8}There was a dispute over some
money my grandmother left.

129
00:06:07,030 --> 00:06:08,657
{\an8}She'd wanted it to go
to the museum.

130
00:06:08,866 --> 00:06:09,908
{\an8}We were working it out.

131
00:06:12,035 --> 00:06:15,455
{\an8}I do know he was very excited
about some new project at work.

132
00:06:16,707 --> 00:06:18,458
- Is this your newspaper?
- No.

133
00:06:18,667 --> 00:06:20,127
- No?
- No.

134
00:06:21,712 --> 00:06:23,505
Okay. You can go.

135
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:29,469
{\an8}Thank you, Curtis.
We'll be in touch.

136
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:31,430
{\an8}Thank you.
Good day to you.

137
00:06:32,306 --> 00:06:35,601
{\an8}Mm. Oh, he's a prime suspect.

138
00:06:36,685 --> 00:06:37,728
Say again?

139
00:06:37,853 --> 00:06:38,979
Oh, I was talking to my colleagues.

140
00:06:40,564 --> 00:06:42,649
Bye, Curtis.
Thank you.

141
00:06:48,864 --> 00:06:50,365
{\an8}Well, he is, you know?

142
00:06:51,116 --> 00:06:54,411
{\an8}Beady eyes, a bespoke suit,
the feud over granny's will

143
00:06:54,536 --> 00:06:55,579
{\an8}Prime suspect.

144
00:06:55,704 --> 00:06:58,081
{\an8}I am gonna find a cup of tea,
sit down and relax

145
00:06:58,206 --> 00:07:00,459
{\an8}and read this newspaper.
I'll call you later.

146
00:07:00,584 --> 00:07:02,961
But wait a second. We're going
to Cartesian, his employer.

147
00:07:03,086 --> 00:07:04,129
Shouldn't you come with us?

148
00:07:04,379 --> 00:07:07,799
{\an8}Maybe later. I'll, uh...
I'll call you if I need you.

149
00:07:08,675 --> 00:07:09,718
{\an8}"If you ne..."

150
00:07:54,054 --> 00:07:57,599
{\an8}Hi. Avery Schultz.
I'm Gabriel's executive assistant.

151
00:07:57,724 --> 00:07:58,809
I can take you to see him now.

152
00:07:59,226 --> 00:08:00,519
{\an8}Um, I'm sorry,
but you'll have to leave

153
00:08:00,644 --> 00:08:02,354
{\an8}your cellphones
and your guns here.

154
00:08:02,479 --> 00:08:03,689
{\an8}- If, if you have guns.
- I see.

155
00:08:03,897 --> 00:08:05,482
We do, and, uh,
no, we're okay.

156
00:08:05,607 --> 00:08:08,777
Oh, it's strict company policy,
for security reasons.

157
00:08:08,944 --> 00:08:10,028
{\an8}Sweetheart, we're FBI.

158
00:08:10,529 --> 00:08:11,571
{\an8}I'm not gonna give up
my Beretta

159
00:08:11,697 --> 00:08:13,573
and my phone
contains protected data.

160
00:08:13,699 --> 00:08:14,825
Our stuff stays with us.

161
00:08:15,242 --> 00:08:16,994
Um, okay.

162
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:19,454
I'll... Let me just check
if it's okay.

163
00:08:20,414 --> 00:08:21,456
You do that.

164
00:08:21,707 --> 00:08:23,625
- You have a Beretta?
- Yeah. You?

165
00:08:23,750 --> 00:08:25,252
I carry a Glock 26, but...

166
00:08:28,964 --> 00:08:30,632
- Hello?
- Hey. Teresa?

167
00:08:30,966 --> 00:08:33,468
Osvaldo Ardiles.
Long time, no see.

168
00:08:33,969 --> 00:08:36,138
Hey, Ardiles.
How are you?

169
00:08:36,888 --> 00:08:38,140
How's the private practice?

170
00:08:39,099 --> 00:08:41,226
You know, more work, more money.
Can't complain.

171
00:08:41,351 --> 00:08:42,394
How's the FBI?

172
00:08:42,519 --> 00:08:45,439
Uh, it's the same money,
more work.

173
00:08:45,564 --> 00:08:46,898
Great dental plan, though.

174
00:08:47,024 --> 00:08:50,152
Hey, Teresa, you think, uh
we could meet for dinner tonight?

175
00:08:50,277 --> 00:08:51,528
I have some business
to discuss.

176
00:08:51,987 --> 00:08:54,573
Dinner? Well, I'm, I'm in Houston.

177
00:08:54,990 --> 00:08:56,033
Oh, great.
I'm in Chicago.

178
00:08:56,158 --> 00:08:57,701
Houston is no problem.
I'll take the jet.

179
00:08:58,368 --> 00:09:00,787
You have a jet that you can use
to take me to dinner?

180
00:09:01,121 --> 00:09:02,914
- What's the deal?
- I'd, uh...

181
00:09:03,081 --> 00:09:06,043
- I'd rather discuss it in person.
- This is business, right?

182
00:09:06,209 --> 00:09:07,836
It's not, you know, personal?

183
00:09:08,587 --> 00:09:11,381
Oh, gosh, yeah.
No. Uh, strictly business.

184
00:09:11,673 --> 00:09:12,966
Well, okay, then.
You're buying.

185
00:09:13,467 --> 00:09:14,509
Deal.

186
00:09:18,972 --> 00:09:20,015
That was weird.

187
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:21,600
Sounds intriguing.
Is there a problem?

188
00:09:21,892 --> 00:09:23,101
- I hope not.
- Okay.

189
00:09:23,477 --> 00:09:25,395
Gabriel said it's okay
to keep your things

190
00:09:25,604 --> 00:09:26,855
so right this way.

191
00:09:28,273 --> 00:09:29,608
It's really sad about Charles.

192
00:09:29,733 --> 00:09:31,777
I saw him Sunday,
and he seemed totally chill.

193
00:09:31,943 --> 00:09:33,320
What? What do you think
could have happened?

194
00:09:33,528 --> 00:09:34,696
That's what we're here
to find out.

195
00:09:35,030 --> 00:09:38,075
Um, this is our pre-viz department.
Pre-visualization.

196
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:39,618
- It, it's basically...
- We know.

197
00:09:44,164 --> 00:09:45,582
Agents Lisbon and Fischer.

198
00:09:45,999 --> 00:09:48,752
Hi. I'm Gabriel Quinn.
I run this place.

199
00:09:48,919 --> 00:09:50,837
This is Leo Drembelas,
my general counsel.

200
00:09:50,962 --> 00:09:52,005
Well, please.

201
00:09:52,464 --> 00:09:53,507
Do you need anything else?

202
00:09:53,715 --> 00:09:55,050
No, we're, we're fine, Avery.
Thank you.

203
00:09:56,301 --> 00:09:57,886
Sorry about the security rigmarole.

204
00:09:58,011 --> 00:10:00,305
We take every precaution we can.

205
00:10:01,807 --> 00:10:02,849
So, how can we help you?

206
00:10:03,016 --> 00:10:04,434
I understand you think
Whitaker was murdered.

207
00:10:04,976 --> 00:10:06,019
We can't rule it out yet.

208
00:10:06,561 --> 00:10:10,023
But suicide or an accidental
overdose is more likely, no?

209
00:10:11,358 --> 00:10:13,235
Tell us a little bit
about Cartesian.

210
00:10:14,861 --> 00:10:17,948
We develop tactical image visualization
for the armed forces.

211
00:10:18,573 --> 00:10:19,658
Been around about six years.

212
00:10:20,325 --> 00:10:21,785
The intelligence we develop

213
00:10:21,910 --> 00:10:24,412
gets us close to a lot
of bad guys overseas.

214
00:10:24,871 --> 00:10:27,541
Had Whitaker been working on
anything sensitive recently?

215
00:10:27,958 --> 00:10:30,460
Ongoing projects
are strictly need-to-know

216
00:10:30,585 --> 00:10:32,921
but assume that everything
we take on is sensitive.

217
00:10:33,547 --> 00:10:36,716
- How did Whitaker get on here?
- He was a lovable goof.

218
00:10:38,385 --> 00:10:41,513
Super-bright, uh, kind of a big kid

219
00:10:42,556 --> 00:10:44,349
gave people nicknames.
He called me Quinner.

220
00:10:44,683 --> 00:10:46,601
He put it on a t-shirt.
He was that guy.

221
00:10:47,102 --> 00:10:50,355
Irritating sometimes,
but nobody disliked him.

222
00:10:50,689 --> 00:10:52,315
We've pinged
the GPS in his car

223
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:54,192
and it told us
that he was here on Sunday

224
00:10:54,317 --> 00:10:55,485
the day before he died.

225
00:10:55,819 --> 00:10:57,404
A lot of your guys come in
on weekends?

226
00:10:57,654 --> 00:10:59,364
Sure. Many. Yeah.

227
00:11:00,073 --> 00:11:01,825
You don't take this job
because it's easy.

228
00:11:02,117 --> 00:11:04,369
We're going to need to speak
with all of your employees

229
00:11:04,536 --> 00:11:07,330
But we'd like to start with the guys
that were here on Sunday.

230
00:11:09,124 --> 00:11:10,167
Of course.

231
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:12,335
I'll need to sit in,
as legal counsel.

232
00:11:28,727 --> 00:11:29,978
Oh, sorry. Don't...

233
00:11:30,896 --> 00:11:32,564
Hey, Cho, finished grilling
the locals?

234
00:11:32,689 --> 00:11:34,733
'cause, uh, I think
I may have a lead.

235
00:11:35,025 --> 00:11:37,652
Sunday. Yeah.
I was fixing some resolution issues

236
00:11:37,777 --> 00:11:39,738
on our dynamic terrain simulator
uh, but...

237
00:11:39,863 --> 00:11:42,699
- Did you talk to Whitaker?
- Sure.

238
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:46,119
Yeah, you know,
"Hey. How's it going?"

239
00:11:46,411 --> 00:11:48,496
- Like that.
- How did he seem?

240
00:11:49,539 --> 00:11:52,375
He was one of those guys...
He was quiet or loud.

241
00:11:52,709 --> 00:11:55,921
Sunday he was quiet,
like, thinking.

242
00:11:56,421 --> 00:11:59,299
His brother said that he was
excited about a new project.

243
00:12:00,842 --> 00:12:03,720
- A new project?
- Why is that strange?

244
00:12:07,098 --> 00:12:08,141
There was none.

245
00:12:08,475 --> 00:12:11,394
Uh, he was stuck on this massive
geo-spatial analysis report.

246
00:12:12,062 --> 00:12:13,813
It's the last thing in the world
you'd be excited about.

247
00:12:14,147 --> 00:12:16,650
And that's all we're at liberty
to divulge about that.

248
00:12:17,192 --> 00:12:19,611
And did you see Whitaker
talk to anybody else on Sunday?

249
00:12:21,071 --> 00:12:23,031
Yeah, he asked Hagen
a lot of questions.

250
00:12:23,949 --> 00:12:26,284
Martin Hagen,
our military liaison.

251
00:12:28,036 --> 00:12:29,246
And what did Whitaker say
to you?

252
00:12:30,372 --> 00:12:32,415
He wanted to know whether
any of the secret technologies

253
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:34,251
developed here
has shown up in foreign hands.

254
00:12:34,417 --> 00:12:36,253
- And what did you say?
- The answer is yes

255
00:12:36,378 --> 00:12:38,171
but there's always
a certain amount of leakage.

256
00:12:38,463 --> 00:12:39,798
And did he tell you
that he thought

257
00:12:39,923 --> 00:12:41,925
somebody inside the company
was stealing data?

258
00:12:42,509 --> 00:12:44,678
- He didn't say that.
- But you got that impression?

259
00:12:44,886 --> 00:12:48,473
With all due respect,
Mr. Hagen's opinion is not germane.

260
00:12:49,432 --> 00:12:50,976
Let me point out, agents

261
00:12:51,810 --> 00:12:54,229
there is zero evidence
of a security breach here.

262
00:12:54,437 --> 00:12:56,398
We cannot even be sure
a murder was committed.

263
00:12:56,523 --> 00:12:58,650
Let us keep this matter
in proportion.

264
00:13:01,903 --> 00:13:04,781
- This is a lead?
- It, it's a coded message. See?

265
00:13:06,157 --> 00:13:07,200
That says what?

266
00:13:08,118 --> 00:13:09,369
I have no idea.

267
00:13:10,453 --> 00:13:11,579
I thought you were good at code-breaking.

268
00:13:11,705 --> 00:13:12,872
Yeah, that's not the point.
The point is

269
00:13:12,998 --> 00:13:15,166
- there's a coded message here.
- Or doodles.

270
00:13:16,126 --> 00:13:17,168
Oh, come on.

271
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:19,462
You know, you...
you've read crime novels.

272
00:13:20,463 --> 00:13:21,923
You, you wanna communicate
with someone

273
00:13:22,048 --> 00:13:23,425
but not be seen with them

274
00:13:24,134 --> 00:13:25,593
then you put a message
in a newspaper

275
00:13:25,719 --> 00:13:26,845
and you leave it
in a public place.

276
00:13:26,970 --> 00:13:30,140
- You think Whitaker was a spy.
- No. I think he found a spy.

277
00:13:30,557 --> 00:13:33,560
He saw someone leave this paper,
and he intercepted the drop.

278
00:13:33,977 --> 00:13:36,479
We need to find out when and where
he found this newspaper.

279
00:13:36,688 --> 00:13:39,607
Espionage is a big leap from
doodles in some old newspaper.

280
00:13:40,191 --> 00:13:42,694
Remember, it's still perfectly possible
that Whitaker killed himself.

281
00:13:43,445 --> 00:13:45,989
The company Whitaker worked for
did some top-secret stuff.

282
00:13:46,614 --> 00:13:49,784
His apartment looked like
it was decorated by an 8-year-old.

283
00:13:50,452 --> 00:13:53,621
He was naive, inquisitive,
very clever

284
00:13:54,622 --> 00:13:58,335
and he had one copy of a very serious
newspaper he never read

285
00:13:58,543 --> 00:14:01,504
which, as you can see,
is faded and stained

286
00:14:01,629 --> 00:14:03,298
as though it's been left
outside someplace.

287
00:14:04,299 --> 00:14:05,342
Now he's dead.

288
00:14:06,843 --> 00:14:09,637
Okay, I have the security
footage from Cartesian.

289
00:14:10,221 --> 00:14:11,473
The newspaper came out
last Friday

290
00:14:11,598 --> 00:14:13,099
so that's our starting point, obviously.

291
00:14:15,894 --> 00:14:18,855
Whitaker arrives at work
at 8:45.

292
00:14:19,356 --> 00:14:20,690
No paper visible.

293
00:14:23,985 --> 00:14:27,489
At 1:15, he comes out,
probably for lunch.

294
00:14:28,239 --> 00:14:29,282
No paper.

295
00:14:30,867 --> 00:14:31,910
Aha!

296
00:14:32,035 --> 00:14:34,496
He comes back at 2:10
with a newspaper.

297
00:14:38,917 --> 00:14:40,335
The financial record

298
00:14:40,919 --> 00:14:43,254
which he puts away inside his jacket
before entering the building.

299
00:14:43,463 --> 00:14:44,506
Aha.

300
00:14:45,423 --> 00:14:46,633
Which way
does he come from?

301
00:14:47,425 --> 00:14:48,843
We'll retrace his steps.

302
00:14:50,845 --> 00:14:53,306
- Uh, he came from the south.
- Thanks, Wylie.

303
00:15:01,356 --> 00:15:02,399
South.

304
00:15:06,653 --> 00:15:07,695
How far south do we walk?

305
00:15:08,029 --> 00:15:11,324
Eventually we'll come to a small park
with a bench under a fig tree.

306
00:15:11,950 --> 00:15:12,992
Why is that?

307
00:15:13,243 --> 00:15:16,079
Bird poop and tree sap,
with a faint figgy note.

308
00:15:17,872 --> 00:15:18,915
No, I'm good.

309
00:15:25,922 --> 00:15:31,010
Ah, look. Ficus Rubiginosa.
It's a fig tree.

310
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:33,638
Okay. Good call, maybe.

311
00:15:34,431 --> 00:15:35,473
And...

312
00:15:36,307 --> 00:15:37,350
Yep.

313
00:15:40,353 --> 00:15:41,396
That's our bench.

314
00:15:42,439 --> 00:15:43,731
What, you don't know the Latin
for bench?

315
00:15:44,816 --> 00:15:46,985
No need to be snippy
just because I was right.

316
00:15:47,652 --> 00:15:49,112
I said good call already.
What now?

317
00:15:49,571 --> 00:15:50,613
Well, it's too late today.

318
00:15:50,738 --> 00:15:52,115
We come back tomorrow
at lunchtime

319
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:53,908
leave the newspaper,
see who picks it up.

320
00:15:54,033 --> 00:15:55,660
- If someone picks it up.
- Oh, someone will.

321
00:15:56,119 --> 00:15:58,204
I suspect the other spy
comes by once a day

322
00:15:58,329 --> 00:16:00,165
to see if there's been
a message left.

323
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:03,168
He'll keep coming until he finds
a newspaper to pick up.

324
00:16:04,335 --> 00:16:05,503
Or Whitaker just OD'd.

325
00:16:05,837 --> 00:16:07,755
Any other brilliant breakthroughs?
Otherwise, I'm gonna hit the gym.

326
00:16:09,382 --> 00:16:10,425
- You should hit the gym.
- Yeah.

327
00:16:13,344 --> 00:16:14,471
- Jane?
- "Lisbon?"

328
00:16:14,888 --> 00:16:15,930
Where are you?

329
00:16:16,264 --> 00:16:18,349
"I'm on the scent of murder
like a bloodhound."

330
00:16:18,475 --> 00:16:19,767
We just questioned the staff.

331
00:16:19,934 --> 00:16:21,603
Apparently, the day before he died

332
00:16:21,728 --> 00:16:24,564
Whitaker was asking people
about industrial espionage.

333
00:16:25,064 --> 00:16:26,524
Yeah, I'm a little ahead of you
on that one.

334
00:16:27,650 --> 00:16:28,735
You broke into my car.

335
00:16:28,943 --> 00:16:31,196
I entered your car
without your knowledge.

336
00:16:31,321 --> 00:16:33,698
But the good news is,
tomorrow at around 2:00

337
00:16:33,823 --> 00:16:35,492
this case will be cracked.

338
00:16:36,117 --> 00:16:38,661
- You sound awfully sure of yourself.
- Always.

339
00:16:38,786 --> 00:16:39,913
So, what's going on tonight?

340
00:16:40,580 --> 00:16:43,208
Tonight I plan on painting
Houston red.

341
00:16:43,333 --> 00:16:44,834
Mm. Where's Cho?

342
00:16:45,043 --> 00:16:47,212
Arm-wrestling with some heavy objects
at the gymnasium.

343
00:16:47,378 --> 00:16:48,963
Oh, I just want room service.

344
00:16:49,130 --> 00:16:50,590
I happen to have a date.

345
00:16:51,549 --> 00:16:52,592
Sorry? A date?

346
00:16:52,842 --> 00:16:55,011
What, like I couldn't possibly
have a date with somebody?

347
00:16:55,512 --> 00:16:56,638
You have a date in Houston?

348
00:16:56,763 --> 00:16:58,806
Yeah. Remember Osvaldo Ardiles?

349
00:16:59,057 --> 00:17:02,769
Ardiles? Two words:
Tassled Loafers.

350
00:17:02,894 --> 00:17:04,938
- Uh, he's not a bad-looking man.
- Tassled Loafers?

351
00:17:05,063 --> 00:17:06,314
Oh, wait.
Hold on. Back up.

352
00:17:06,439 --> 00:17:07,482
You said you already
solved the case.

353
00:17:07,607 --> 00:17:08,775
Are you gonna tell us how?

354
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:10,026
Oh, ask him,
and he won't tell you.

355
00:17:10,151 --> 00:17:11,986
Pretend you're not interested,
and he might.

356
00:17:12,612 --> 00:17:13,780
I'm not even gonna try.

357
00:17:14,197 --> 00:17:17,534
Uh, I... I'm sorry. You...
You said, uh, "date"

358
00:17:17,659 --> 00:17:18,785
and then the subject changed.

359
00:17:19,202 --> 00:17:22,747
Yeah, okay. It's not exactly a date date.
It's a business thing.

360
00:17:22,872 --> 00:17:25,166
He is flying in
on a private jet from Chicago.

361
00:17:25,291 --> 00:17:27,210
Wow, that's very impressive. Why?

362
00:17:28,086 --> 00:17:29,504
I don't know.
I'll find out tonight.

363
00:17:41,806 --> 00:17:43,474
So, how's life
in San Francisco?

364
00:17:44,559 --> 00:17:46,227
Oh, it's good.
I'm barely there.

365
00:17:46,561 --> 00:17:48,062
This case has me traveling
half the month.

366
00:17:48,646 --> 00:17:51,232
- Big case.
- Yeah, defending a cellphone company.

367
00:17:51,774 --> 00:17:54,026
Competitor says my client
stole their phone design

368
00:17:54,151 --> 00:17:56,904
so my client's looking at a hundred
million dollars in legal bills

369
00:17:57,029 --> 00:17:58,072
give or take.

370
00:17:58,197 --> 00:17:59,448
I'd be happy to take some.

371
00:18:01,409 --> 00:18:02,451
Listen, the, um...

372
00:18:04,287 --> 00:18:07,331
The thing is
several times now

373
00:18:08,457 --> 00:18:13,588
in meetings, uh, my phone gets hot

374
00:18:14,213 --> 00:18:15,298
even though I haven't used it

375
00:18:15,506 --> 00:18:17,967
you know, like it's been on
the whole time

376
00:18:18,092 --> 00:18:22,805
like, like somebody hacked it
and used it to listen in on me.

377
00:18:24,348 --> 00:18:27,435
Ardiles, you flew here
all the way from Chicago

378
00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:28,894
to tell me
your phone gets hot?

379
00:18:30,396 --> 00:18:31,439
Yeah.

380
00:18:31,772 --> 00:18:33,149
What is it
you're not telling me?

381
00:18:35,943 --> 00:18:37,403
Nothing. Nothing verifiable.

382
00:18:39,572 --> 00:18:40,615
Go on.

383
00:18:41,365 --> 00:18:42,408
Last couple months

384
00:18:44,869 --> 00:18:46,704
I just get this bad,
bad feeling.

385
00:18:47,872 --> 00:18:49,457
You know,
like somebody is watching me

386
00:18:49,582 --> 00:18:51,667
somebody malicious,
who really doesn't like me.

387
00:18:51,792 --> 00:18:52,835
You ever get that feeling?

388
00:18:53,502 --> 00:18:56,297
I'm sure your company has
good detectives on retainer.

389
00:18:56,422 --> 00:18:57,882
Yeah, but I can't go to them

390
00:18:58,341 --> 00:19:00,092
'cause I don't think it has
anything to do with my business.

391
00:19:00,217 --> 00:19:02,136
Why not?
Uh, you said it was a big case.

392
00:19:02,261 --> 00:19:03,846
No, this is not the way
the other side plays.

393
00:19:03,971 --> 00:19:05,264
This is personal, all right?

394
00:19:05,389 --> 00:19:07,558
But I got no...
I got no real proof.

395
00:19:07,683 --> 00:19:09,185
You know,
I sound like a crazy person.

396
00:19:13,564 --> 00:19:15,191
This is not
in my head, Teresa.

397
00:19:16,150 --> 00:19:17,193
Come on.
You know me.

398
00:19:18,444 --> 00:19:19,779
There is something going on

399
00:19:19,904 --> 00:19:22,823
and I need the advice
of a detective

400
00:19:22,948 --> 00:19:24,742
that I can trust 100%.

401
00:19:27,203 --> 00:19:30,247
As it happens, I know
exactly what you should do.

402
00:19:31,290 --> 00:19:33,042
Uh, when you go back
to San Francisco

403
00:19:33,167 --> 00:19:35,211
call Wayne Rigsby
and Grace Van Pelt.

404
00:19:35,336 --> 00:19:36,545
They, they're in the bay area

405
00:19:36,671 --> 00:19:38,589
and what you need
is what they do now.

406
00:19:38,798 --> 00:19:41,008
Digital security.
They're good.

407
00:19:41,133 --> 00:19:43,052
If there's anything going on,
they'll find out.

408
00:19:46,806 --> 00:19:48,683
Drink up.
Hot phones.

409
00:19:51,977 --> 00:19:54,730
"He seemed a bit jumpy.
But he was always a little jumpy."

410
00:19:55,064 --> 00:19:57,358
I think he might be losing it,
but you never know.

411
00:19:57,692 --> 00:19:59,026
Maybe it really is bugged.

412
00:20:00,152 --> 00:20:02,029
Anyway, Rigsby and Van pelt
could use the business.

413
00:20:02,655 --> 00:20:04,115
Maybe he just wanted a date
with you.

414
00:20:05,032 --> 00:20:06,992
- Have you considered that?
- Please stop.

415
00:20:07,410 --> 00:20:09,203
Ardiles. He always had
a crush on you.

416
00:20:12,123 --> 00:20:14,458
It's 2:23.
This is a waste of time.

417
00:20:14,750 --> 00:20:16,752
Your theoretical spy
isn't showing up.

418
00:20:16,961 --> 00:20:19,547
- You have to have patience, Lisbon.
- I'd rather have coffee.

419
00:20:20,005 --> 00:20:21,507
There's a place over there.
You want anything?

420
00:20:21,716 --> 00:20:22,758
- I'm good.
- Okay.

421
00:21:09,805 --> 00:21:12,016
Uh, excuse me, sir.
Do you happen to have the time?

422
00:21:13,476 --> 00:21:16,145
- You have a watch.
- Yeah, I, I forgot to wind it.

423
00:21:19,148 --> 00:21:22,193
Uh, it's almost 2:25.

424
00:21:22,526 --> 00:21:23,736
- Thank you.
- No problem.

425
00:21:24,278 --> 00:21:26,030
- Did you take my phone?
- What?

426
00:21:26,155 --> 00:21:27,573
I can't...
I can't find my phone.

427
00:21:27,823 --> 00:21:29,366
No, I didn't take anything
from you.

428
00:21:29,533 --> 00:21:32,119
- Then where is my phone?
- How would I know?

429
00:21:32,495 --> 00:21:33,913
Hey, hey, whoa, whoa.
Wait a minute.

430
00:21:34,038 --> 00:21:35,456
Help. Police! Police!

431
00:21:36,040 --> 00:21:38,334
- This man stole my phone.
- What's going on?

432
00:21:39,168 --> 00:21:40,836
He stopped me
and he asked me the time

433
00:21:40,961 --> 00:21:42,546
and he just snatched my phone
right out of my hand.

434
00:21:42,671 --> 00:21:43,756
- He's a thief.
- He is lying.

435
00:21:43,881 --> 00:21:46,133
He stopped me,
he asked for the time, and I...

436
00:21:46,258 --> 00:21:48,844
- Sir, sir, is that true?
- No, sir.

437
00:21:49,553 --> 00:21:51,806
He stopped me,
he asked for the time, and...

438
00:21:52,181 --> 00:21:54,058
- This man is crazy.
- Uh, liar.

439
00:21:54,600 --> 00:21:56,268
Could you call my phone, please?

440
00:21:56,685 --> 00:21:59,855
- Call.
- 555-0189.

441
00:22:05,820 --> 00:22:07,071
Yes. There. Thank you.

442
00:22:07,404 --> 00:22:08,989
That's my phone.
That's my ringtone.

443
00:22:15,162 --> 00:22:16,205
Very good.

444
00:22:16,580 --> 00:22:17,623
You tricked me.

445
00:22:18,499 --> 00:22:21,210
- Yeah. Thank you.
- Very good. Very good.

446
00:22:21,460 --> 00:22:23,170
Oh, you, you're gonna let him
just walk away?

447
00:22:23,295 --> 00:22:25,089
Sir, sir, I didn't tell you
you could leave.

448
00:22:26,423 --> 00:22:29,093
- What's happening?
- A whole lot is happening.

449
00:22:37,101 --> 00:22:41,188
So, Mr. Hassan Zarif,
you're in quite the pickle.

450
00:22:42,314 --> 00:22:45,025
Please, before you waste
valuable breath

451
00:22:46,819 --> 00:22:48,445
I will not boondoggle
with the FBI.

452
00:22:49,864 --> 00:22:50,906
what do you want to know?

453
00:22:52,908 --> 00:22:55,911
How long have you been buying
intelligence from inside Cartesian?

454
00:22:56,579 --> 00:22:58,956
Uh, a year, about.

455
00:23:00,583 --> 00:23:02,918
- From who?
- I do not know.

456
00:23:03,836 --> 00:23:06,046
My client arranged the first purchase.

457
00:23:06,839 --> 00:23:07,882
And who's your client?

458
00:23:09,675 --> 00:23:13,804
They're far away abroad
and very powerful and very rich.

459
00:23:15,014 --> 00:23:16,765
And I will never tell you
their names

460
00:23:18,142 --> 00:23:19,226
Even if you torture me.

461
00:23:20,603 --> 00:23:22,897
Mm. Okay.

462
00:23:23,981 --> 00:23:26,066
So, tell us about the seller
inside Cartesian.

463
00:23:27,234 --> 00:23:31,280
I know nothing. I mean,
all contact is anonymous.

464
00:23:31,989 --> 00:23:36,035
When the seller has a file to sell,
he drops a newspaper at the park.

465
00:23:38,037 --> 00:23:39,163
We have a simple code.

466
00:23:39,496 --> 00:23:42,583
This one says
"same price as last time."

467
00:23:43,208 --> 00:23:44,251
Then what happens?

468
00:23:45,002 --> 00:23:48,547
Um, we have another spot
in the same park

469
00:23:48,881 --> 00:23:49,965
for the actual exchange.

470
00:23:51,634 --> 00:23:54,970
I leave cash.
The seller picks it up

471
00:23:55,346 --> 00:23:58,432
leaves a flash drive
with the data.

472
00:23:58,557 --> 00:24:02,144
I, I paid $475,000 in total.

473
00:24:02,686 --> 00:24:03,729
Ouch.

474
00:24:05,564 --> 00:24:07,191
But you have no idea
who this person is?

475
00:24:08,609 --> 00:24:09,735
No, sir, I do not.

476
00:24:12,279 --> 00:24:14,657
- Do you know a Charles Whitaker?
- No.

477
00:24:16,033 --> 00:24:18,369
He worked at Cartesian
died two days ago

478
00:24:18,661 --> 00:24:19,703
heart failure.

479
00:24:21,956 --> 00:24:23,624
May he rest peacefully.

480
00:24:24,833 --> 00:24:26,835
- What of it?
- We think he was murdered

481
00:24:27,378 --> 00:24:31,548
either by your client or you
or whoever you're buying from.

482
00:24:33,342 --> 00:24:34,385
I see.

483
00:24:36,095 --> 00:24:39,264
- That puts a different complexion.
- Yeah.

484
00:24:40,099 --> 00:24:41,141
Very sad.

485
00:24:41,934 --> 00:24:45,062
Uh, my client
did not kill this person.

486
00:24:45,312 --> 00:24:46,355
I assure you.

487
00:24:46,939 --> 00:24:49,191
Uh, the arrangement was too lucrative.

488
00:24:49,316 --> 00:24:51,652
I, I did not do it
for the same reason. I mean

489
00:24:52,736 --> 00:24:53,779
you must believe me.

490
00:24:56,198 --> 00:24:57,241
Yes, we must.

491
00:24:59,535 --> 00:25:01,328
The spy inside Cartesian
is the killer

492
00:25:01,453 --> 00:25:03,622
and I have an excellent plan
for their capture.

493
00:25:03,831 --> 00:25:04,873
Which is what?

494
00:25:05,082 --> 00:25:06,875
Well, our mystery spy
had something to sell.

495
00:25:07,001 --> 00:25:09,503
That's why they left a newspaper
but Whitaker screwed it up.

496
00:25:10,379 --> 00:25:12,006
So they still have
something to sell.

497
00:25:12,131 --> 00:25:14,049
And they don't know
we intercepted their buyer.

498
00:25:14,174 --> 00:25:15,509
Exactly. They'll try again.

499
00:25:15,926 --> 00:25:18,595
They just need to be lulled
into a false sense of security.

500
00:25:18,721 --> 00:25:20,431
And how do we lull them
like that?

501
00:25:20,806 --> 00:25:21,890
We need to arrest
someone else

502
00:25:22,057 --> 00:25:23,434
and charge them
with Whitaker's murder.

503
00:25:23,642 --> 00:25:26,395
We can't arrest people
under false pretenses.

504
00:25:26,520 --> 00:25:27,563
- We can't?
- No.

505
00:25:27,688 --> 00:25:28,731
- No.
- Why not?

506
00:25:29,064 --> 00:25:30,107
It's illegal.

507
00:25:30,858 --> 00:25:32,526
Oh. Well, that's a problem.

508
00:25:32,943 --> 00:25:34,737
I guess we need to think
outside of the box.

509
00:25:35,362 --> 00:25:36,655
That's always a bad sign.

510
00:25:48,167 --> 00:25:51,170
- Relax.
- I am relaxed.

511
00:25:52,129 --> 00:25:54,757
Really? I've seen rodeo bulls
more relaxed.

512
00:25:56,633 --> 00:25:57,676
Okay, I'm not relaxed.

513
00:25:59,261 --> 00:26:00,763
You're acting like you used to act
in the early days

514
00:26:00,888 --> 00:26:01,930
and it makes me nervous.

515
00:26:02,556 --> 00:26:04,475
- How did I act in the early days?
- You were crazy.

516
00:26:05,851 --> 00:26:07,728
Oh. I'm not anymore.

517
00:26:08,896 --> 00:26:10,022
- You're not?
- Nope.

518
00:26:10,606 --> 00:26:11,648
Then what are you?

519
00:26:13,484 --> 00:26:14,526
I'm fine.

520
00:26:15,569 --> 00:26:16,904
I know how to calibrate
these things.

521
00:26:19,907 --> 00:26:21,867
Ah. Mr. Whitaker.

522
00:26:23,535 --> 00:26:25,621
Mr. Jane.
How can I help you?

523
00:26:25,746 --> 00:26:27,498
The question is,
how may I help you?

524
00:26:28,457 --> 00:26:29,500
I don't follow.

525
00:26:29,625 --> 00:26:31,168
Well, as you can imagine,
Mr. Whitaker

526
00:26:31,293 --> 00:26:33,295
I've been watching you
very hard.

527
00:26:33,754 --> 00:26:35,047
Very, very hard.

528
00:26:36,048 --> 00:26:38,592
I know what you did.
Oh, yeah, I know.

529
00:26:39,551 --> 00:26:40,677
What do you mean?
What did I do?

530
00:26:40,803 --> 00:26:42,096
You want me to say it
out loud?

531
00:26:42,888 --> 00:26:45,265
Most people would pay me
to keep quiet.

532
00:26:45,974 --> 00:26:47,226
Are you asking me
for a bribe?

533
00:26:52,606 --> 00:26:53,649
How dare you?

534
00:26:54,817 --> 00:26:56,652
- Oh, my god!
- Is that gonna do?

535
00:26:57,236 --> 00:26:59,321
Sir, you're under arrest
for battery.

536
00:26:59,613 --> 00:27:02,032
Samuels, call someone!
This is ridiculous.

537
00:27:03,408 --> 00:27:04,451
What is happening?

538
00:27:12,791 --> 00:27:13,834
- Lawyer.
- Curtis.

539
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,461
Call me Mr. Whitaker.
And I want to speak with my attorney.

540
00:27:16,711 --> 00:27:18,880
Mr. Whitaker,
you don't need a lawyer.

541
00:27:19,005 --> 00:27:20,048
Damn right.

542
00:27:20,173 --> 00:27:21,508
Any clown on the street
could tell you

543
00:27:21,716 --> 00:27:23,343
that battery charge won't stick.

544
00:27:23,927 --> 00:27:27,139
I was grossly insulted
by that, that man

545
00:27:27,264 --> 00:27:29,057
- and I was falsely arrested.
- You're right.

546
00:27:29,182 --> 00:27:30,976
It was a pretext
to get you off the street

547
00:27:31,101 --> 00:27:33,395
- in a conspicuous way.
- What?

548
00:27:34,771 --> 00:27:37,232
I'm very sorry for any trouble
that you've been caused.

549
00:27:37,649 --> 00:27:39,442
- We need your help.
- My help with what?

550
00:27:39,818 --> 00:27:42,279
We think that Charles
was hunting for a spy at work.

551
00:27:42,529 --> 00:27:44,990
Hunting for a spy?
Charles made maps.

552
00:27:45,115 --> 00:27:47,033
Right, but the company
that he worked for

553
00:27:47,242 --> 00:27:50,203
they're a Pentagon subcontractor
Battlefield apps.

554
00:27:50,495 --> 00:27:53,290
Somebody was stealing
and selling sensitive military data.

555
00:27:53,415 --> 00:27:54,583
Charles caught on to it.

556
00:27:54,708 --> 00:27:57,002
Sounds exactly like
the kind of half-baked fantasy

557
00:27:57,127 --> 00:27:58,170
Charles would have cooked up.

558
00:27:58,295 --> 00:27:59,337
Except it's true.

559
00:27:59,796 --> 00:28:02,048
You're saying my brother got
killed for rooting out a spy?

560
00:28:02,174 --> 00:28:04,050
- Yes.
- And now you need my help?

561
00:28:04,217 --> 00:28:06,845
We need his killer to feel safe
enough to make one more deal.

562
00:28:07,470 --> 00:28:09,181
We need them to think
no one's looking at them

563
00:28:09,306 --> 00:28:10,640
so we need someone else
to look at

564
00:28:11,433 --> 00:28:12,475
someone plausible.

565
00:28:13,560 --> 00:28:14,895
- Me.
- Exactly.

566
00:28:15,103 --> 00:28:16,438
That's an absurd idea.

567
00:28:16,563 --> 00:28:18,857
Is this really
how the FBI operates? Really?

568
00:28:19,357 --> 00:28:22,152
- We're a specialized unit.
- What if I say no?

569
00:28:22,360 --> 00:28:24,029
Then we'll press
the battery charges.

570
00:28:24,362 --> 00:28:25,947
But you just admitted
those were bogus.

571
00:28:26,072 --> 00:28:27,115
Only to you.

572
00:28:27,741 --> 00:28:28,867
Either way,
you're staying here.

573
00:28:29,117 --> 00:28:30,243
This is outrageous.

574
00:28:30,827 --> 00:28:32,370
- I swear, my attorneys will...
- Curtis.

575
00:28:33,455 --> 00:28:34,497
This isn't about you.

576
00:28:35,123 --> 00:28:38,418
This is about national security
and justice for your brother.

577
00:28:39,419 --> 00:28:40,462
So deal with it.

578
00:28:48,011 --> 00:28:50,639
As a courtesy, we wanted to update
you on the Whitaker case.

579
00:28:51,014 --> 00:28:53,058
We saw the news.
You arrested his brother?

580
00:28:53,642 --> 00:28:56,144
Yes. Uh, we haven't charged him yet,
but soon.

581
00:28:56,269 --> 00:28:59,147
There was a dispute over
some money that a relative left.

582
00:28:59,481 --> 00:29:01,233
Curtis got greedy,
it looks like.

583
00:29:01,441 --> 00:29:03,235
I didn't know there was money
in the family.

584
00:29:03,610 --> 00:29:04,653
Several million.

585
00:29:05,278 --> 00:29:07,113
Is there anything else
you need from us, agents?

586
00:29:07,239 --> 00:29:09,741
No, no. We just wanted you
to hear it from us.

587
00:29:09,950 --> 00:29:11,910
And we know Charles had a lot
of friends in the building.

588
00:29:14,371 --> 00:29:15,997
I just can't believe it.
It's so sad.

589
00:29:16,623 --> 00:29:19,084
It's very sad,
but family feuds run deep.

590
00:29:19,376 --> 00:29:20,835
We see this sort of thing
all the time.

591
00:29:21,127 --> 00:29:23,421
Yeah, of course.
It's just... Wow.

592
00:29:24,089 --> 00:29:25,757
Well, have a safe trip
back to Austin.

593
00:29:31,137 --> 00:29:33,223
And you haven't made any phone calls
since the incident?

594
00:29:33,890 --> 00:29:36,101
On my cell? No.
I bought a burner.

595
00:29:36,226 --> 00:29:39,896
Can you believe that? Me in some grimy back
alley buying a prepaid phone

596
00:29:40,021 --> 00:29:41,523
from a kid
who probably stole the thing.

597
00:29:41,856 --> 00:29:45,110
Oh, that was smart. If, in fact, your
digital life has been compromised.

598
00:29:45,777 --> 00:29:47,904
You two can do this?
You can take over someone's phone?

599
00:29:48,905 --> 00:29:49,948
Yeah.

600
00:29:50,073 --> 00:29:51,908
'cause whoever's doing this
to me, I wanna nail them.

601
00:29:52,033 --> 00:29:53,243
I wanna destroy them.

602
00:29:54,494 --> 00:29:56,663
I'm sorry.
This one will not go to sleep.

603
00:29:57,122 --> 00:29:59,207
No, I'm, uh, I'm sorry.
I got excited.

604
00:30:00,458 --> 00:30:02,585
Look, we can't wage war
on whoever bugged your phone.

605
00:30:02,711 --> 00:30:03,753
If you were bugged.

606
00:30:03,878 --> 00:30:05,672
That's just not a service
that we provide.

607
00:30:06,298 --> 00:30:07,340
I understand.

608
00:30:07,507 --> 00:30:08,717
We'll start
by looking into your phone

609
00:30:08,842 --> 00:30:10,468
and as soon as we know anything,
you'll know.

610
00:30:10,635 --> 00:30:12,971
- Thank you.
- And you understand the pricing schedule?

611
00:30:13,388 --> 00:30:15,390
Yep, I do. It's no problem.
Do whatever it takes.

612
00:30:16,141 --> 00:30:18,977
Look, I'm scared.
And I want this fixed.

613
00:30:20,103 --> 00:30:21,146
All righty, then.

614
00:30:22,272 --> 00:30:23,315
We'll be in touch.

615
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:28,403
Wow.

616
00:30:28,862 --> 00:30:29,904
This is juicy.

617
00:30:30,655 --> 00:30:33,908
But like Lisbon said,
he seemed a little out there.

618
00:30:34,617 --> 00:30:35,869
"What you think
he's imagining this stuff?"

619
00:30:35,994 --> 00:30:37,037
"Well, I hope not."

620
00:30:37,162 --> 00:30:39,622
"I mean, I hope he's not in danger
or anything."

621
00:30:39,748 --> 00:30:42,375
"But this could be a big case
for us."

622
00:30:42,917 --> 00:30:45,879
"It would be too bad if it were just
a faulty phone and paranoia."

623
00:30:54,637 --> 00:30:56,681
{\an8}"2:11. Still no action
at the park benches."

624
00:30:58,516 --> 00:31:01,144
"No sign of anyone approaching
from either side of the Plaza."

625
00:31:01,478 --> 00:31:02,979
No kidding.
Maintain radio silence

626
00:31:03,104 --> 00:31:04,147
unless you have something to say.

627
00:31:04,564 --> 00:31:05,607
"Yes, sir."

628
00:31:06,733 --> 00:31:07,776
You tell 'em, Cho.

629
00:31:09,444 --> 00:31:12,655
Mm. This is like
a stakeout of old.

630
00:31:13,698 --> 00:31:17,118
You, skeptical and grumpy.
Me, confident, cheerful.

631
00:31:17,243 --> 00:31:19,329
You're only being cheerful
to irritate me.

632
00:31:19,746 --> 00:31:21,998
I'm cheerful because
I get to sit here with you

633
00:31:22,374 --> 00:31:24,250
in this park
on such a beautiful day.

634
00:31:24,376 --> 00:31:26,002
Son of a gun.
It's Avery Schultz.

635
00:31:26,127 --> 00:31:27,796
Gesundheit. Is that a real person?

636
00:31:27,962 --> 00:31:29,255
It's the CEO's secretary.

637
00:31:32,884 --> 00:31:34,135
"She's sitting on the target bench."

638
00:31:46,648 --> 00:31:47,690
"She's made the drop."

639
00:31:49,275 --> 00:31:50,985
"Okay, we've seen enough.
Move in."

640
00:31:51,611 --> 00:31:52,654
"Copy that."

641
00:31:53,488 --> 00:31:54,531
"Movin' in."

642
00:31:57,659 --> 00:31:59,285
- "Do you see her?"
- "Yes."

643
00:32:00,203 --> 00:32:02,038
- "Stay on her."
- "Copy that."

644
00:32:04,416 --> 00:32:06,418
- "She's on the move."
- "Go, go, go, go. Let' go."

645
00:32:07,627 --> 00:32:08,670
Stop! FBI!

646
00:32:09,087 --> 00:32:11,464
FBI! Out of the way!
Everyone, move, move!

647
00:32:12,715 --> 00:32:13,758
Stop!

648
00:32:15,635 --> 00:32:16,845
"She's cutting through the fountain."

649
00:32:17,470 --> 00:32:18,513
"Get on the other side."

650
00:32:19,889 --> 00:32:21,766
- Hold it right there!
- Avery Schultz.

651
00:32:23,977 --> 00:32:25,019
You're under arrest.

652
00:32:27,063 --> 00:32:29,482
"Subject is contained. I repeat,
subject is contained."

653
00:32:31,609 --> 00:32:32,652
Come on out of there.

654
00:32:35,196 --> 00:32:36,573
I would have never thought
it was her.

655
00:32:38,199 --> 00:32:39,868
Huh. Really?
You surprise me.

656
00:32:39,993 --> 00:32:41,703
You normally have
such good people instincts.

657
00:32:41,828 --> 00:32:43,455
- I do?
- No, I'm kidding.

658
00:32:44,539 --> 00:32:46,124
Well, I was kidding
that I was kidding.

659
00:32:46,249 --> 00:32:47,625
You have very good
people instincts.

660
00:32:49,210 --> 00:32:51,629
It's just your people skills
are a little...

661
00:32:51,754 --> 00:32:54,007
- Oh, you're hilarious.
- Yeah.

662
00:32:54,799 --> 00:32:55,925
Nicely done, Cho.

663
00:32:57,844 --> 00:32:58,887
You look great in that hat.

664
00:33:08,503 --> 00:33:09,545
We searched your apartment.

665
00:33:11,506 --> 00:33:13,299
Some nice shoes,
but otherwise kind of Spartan.

666
00:33:15,802 --> 00:33:18,054
Your buyer says he paid you
almost $500,000.

667
00:33:19,472 --> 00:33:21,516
My mom has medical issues.

668
00:33:23,017 --> 00:33:24,852
And I have drug issues,
to be frank.

669
00:33:25,603 --> 00:33:26,646
So the money goes.

670
00:33:26,771 --> 00:33:28,398
How'd you get started
in the espionage business?

671
00:33:28,731 --> 00:33:32,193
My dad was a big-deal
currency trader until he went bust.

672
00:33:32,527 --> 00:33:33,569
We lost everything.

673
00:33:34,362 --> 00:33:37,115
Five years ago,
he killed himself.

674
00:33:39,951 --> 00:33:40,993
I was desperate

675
00:33:41,119 --> 00:33:42,703
so a man reached out to me
on the internet

676
00:33:42,829 --> 00:33:44,580
said he was a friend
of my father's

677
00:33:44,789 --> 00:33:46,499
said he was a journalist

678
00:33:47,083 --> 00:33:49,419
said he would pay me for gossip
from the high-tech world.

679
00:33:49,544 --> 00:33:51,712
- It grew from there.
- And what's the name of this man?

680
00:33:52,046 --> 00:33:53,423
Oh, he doesn't exist.

681
00:33:54,173 --> 00:33:58,261
He is a crime syndicate
in Russia someplace, I think.

682
00:33:58,803 --> 00:34:00,221
By the time I realized that,
it was too late.

683
00:34:00,346 --> 00:34:01,389
I had gone too far already.

684
00:34:01,848 --> 00:34:03,015
When I said I wanted to stop

685
00:34:03,182 --> 00:34:05,309
they threatened to tell
Cartesian what I had been doing

686
00:34:05,476 --> 00:34:08,187
so, I had to keep doing it.

687
00:34:10,022 --> 00:34:11,441
Then that idiot
started following me.

688
00:34:11,566 --> 00:34:12,650
Charles Whitaker.

689
00:34:13,943 --> 00:34:15,987
I had a feeling
someone was watching.

690
00:34:17,780 --> 00:34:19,157
"But you do, don't you?"

691
00:34:19,490 --> 00:34:21,451
"When you're doing stuff
like that, you get paranoid."

692
00:34:22,285 --> 00:34:24,120
"So I forced myself
to ignore the feeling."

693
00:34:24,704 --> 00:34:25,746
"Big mistake."

694
00:34:26,122 --> 00:34:27,790
"We were both in the office
on Sunday."

695
00:34:28,166 --> 00:34:29,709
He told me he knew
what was going on.

696
00:34:30,877 --> 00:34:32,962
Avery, we work for the Pentagon.

697
00:34:33,796 --> 00:34:36,799
What you're doing?
They call treason.

698
00:34:37,008 --> 00:34:38,843
- You have to stop.
- Treason?

699
00:34:39,677 --> 00:34:41,846
Charles, it's a game.

700
00:34:42,805 --> 00:34:44,098
It's something we do for fun

701
00:34:44,724 --> 00:34:46,017
me and bunch of friends
from college.

702
00:34:46,142 --> 00:34:48,895
We've been doing it for years.
It's like a scavenger hunt.

703
00:34:49,020 --> 00:34:52,899
- So, you're not selling stuff?
- Me? A spy? Really?

704
00:34:54,567 --> 00:34:55,610
No.

705
00:34:56,694 --> 00:34:57,737
Charles.

706
00:35:00,865 --> 00:35:02,241
It's just a game.

707
00:35:05,077 --> 00:35:07,455
"But I knew that lie
was not gonna hold him for long."

708
00:35:08,122 --> 00:35:09,415
"I knew
I had to keep him quiet."

709
00:35:10,625 --> 00:35:12,126
So I went to see him early Monday.

710
00:35:15,213 --> 00:35:16,255
Hey.

711
00:35:16,714 --> 00:35:18,132
- Can we talk?
- Sure.

712
00:35:18,716 --> 00:35:19,759
Come in, I guess.

713
00:35:37,109 --> 00:35:39,487
"I put enough drugs in him
to kill a horse."

714
00:35:42,073 --> 00:35:44,659
He didn't look it,
but that guy was strong.

715
00:35:45,743 --> 00:35:46,786
Shame you had to kill him.

716
00:35:47,370 --> 00:35:49,539
Hey, you're a cop.
You're not a judge.

717
00:35:50,623 --> 00:35:53,459
Don't judge me.
I had no choice.

718
00:35:53,584 --> 00:35:55,169
- No, you had a choice.
- Yeah

719
00:35:55,920 --> 00:35:58,673
I could have given up.
But I don't give up.

720
00:36:00,675 --> 00:36:03,844
And that is a virtue.
It's not a sin.

721
00:36:15,273 --> 00:36:16,566
- "Wayne?"
- Hey, Grace.

722
00:36:16,732 --> 00:36:18,025
I can't contact Ardiles.

723
00:36:18,317 --> 00:36:19,902
His office hasn't heard from him
since Friday.

724
00:36:20,236 --> 00:36:22,446
Can't exactly call the guy and tell him
his cell phone's been bugged.

725
00:36:22,572 --> 00:36:24,282
"Can you ping his cellphone,
try and figure out where he is?"

726
00:36:24,490 --> 00:36:25,533
Sure thing.

727
00:36:30,246 --> 00:36:31,956
1313 Owens street.

728
00:36:32,248 --> 00:36:35,710
1313 Owens street.

729
00:36:35,918 --> 00:36:36,961
Yeah, got it.

730
00:36:37,837 --> 00:36:39,297
Hey, are you gonna be home
for dinner?

731
00:36:39,422 --> 00:36:40,548
"Uh, yeah, sure."

732
00:36:41,465 --> 00:36:43,342
Wait. Are you at
that dirty taco truck?

733
00:36:46,846 --> 00:36:48,681
I can literally hear you
trying to think up an excuse.

734
00:36:49,181 --> 00:36:51,601
No. That's the sound
of me eating a taco.

735
00:36:52,393 --> 00:36:54,562
Salad tonight. I promise.
I love you. Bye.

736
00:36:56,105 --> 00:36:57,523
Hey, baby.
What are you doing?

737
00:37:04,280 --> 00:37:07,366
The National Intelligence
Distinguished Service Medal

738
00:37:08,534 --> 00:37:12,330
for your brother's selfless contributions
to the intelligence community.

739
00:37:12,580 --> 00:37:13,748
You should be very proud of him.

740
00:37:23,466 --> 00:37:26,052
Now we'll get you
to the presentation room, okay?

741
00:37:26,886 --> 00:37:28,471
Agent Fischer, you coming along?

742
00:37:28,638 --> 00:37:30,306
Cho and Lisbon
are already there.

743
00:37:30,431 --> 00:37:33,434
- Uh, I'll be along in a moment, sir.
- Okay.

744
00:37:41,025 --> 00:37:42,068
Okay, he's gone.

745
00:37:43,527 --> 00:37:45,529
- Scaredy-cat.
- Just being considerate.

746
00:37:46,364 --> 00:37:48,532
Seeing me again
would have spoiled his big day.

747
00:37:49,492 --> 00:37:51,952
Oh, by the way, I just, uh...

748
00:37:52,078 --> 00:37:53,245
I found the trailer I was looking for.

749
00:37:54,497 --> 00:37:56,874
You can have
any vehicle you want

750
00:37:56,999 --> 00:37:59,001
and you choose
an airstream trailer?

751
00:37:59,126 --> 00:38:01,545
But that, it's for
an old retired couple.

752
00:38:01,671 --> 00:38:03,047
I think it's an elegant solution.

753
00:38:03,631 --> 00:38:05,549
An office and a vehicle in one.

754
00:38:06,050 --> 00:38:08,844
Could change my entire approach
to solving crimes.

755
00:38:08,969 --> 00:38:10,304
I won't fight you on that.

756
00:38:11,013 --> 00:38:13,641
Hey, what did you say to him
that made him so angry that he hit you?

757
00:38:13,766 --> 00:38:15,184
Oh, I have a foolproof line.

758
00:38:20,606 --> 00:38:21,649
Foolproof.

759
00:38:38,332 --> 00:38:41,836
1313 Owens street. Okay.

760
00:39:44,523 --> 00:39:45,941
"This is Wayne.
Please leave a message."

761
00:39:46,859 --> 00:39:49,570
Wayne, uh, call me back
as soon as you get this.

762
00:39:50,696 --> 00:39:51,947
Actually, don't call me back.

763
00:39:52,239 --> 00:39:54,950
Just, uh, meet me at the corner
where you had lunch.

764
00:39:55,326 --> 00:39:56,368
I'll be waiting for you.

765
00:39:57,787 --> 00:39:59,038
Don't worry. It's not the kids.

766
00:40:00,080 --> 00:40:03,501
"Ardiles isn't the only one
being bugged. It's all of us."
